---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Swaprika docs, how context aware ingredient substitution works"
  description="How the Swaprika API models substitution: the edge carries the data, contexts have three states, and substitutability does not compose. Quickstart, schema reference and limits."
  page="docs"
>
  <main>
    <div class="docs">
      <aside class="toc">
        <div class="panel-label">On this page</div>
        <a href="#idea">The idea</a>
        <a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a>
        <a href="#auth">Keys and plans</a>
        <a href="#model">How the model works</a>
        <a href="#three-states">Three states, not two</a>
        <a href="#chains">Why there are no chains</a>
        <a href="#schema">Schema reference</a>
        <a href="#limits">Limits and honesty</a>
        <a href="#licence">Licence</a>
      </aside>

      <div class="doc-body">
        <h1>Docs</h1>
        <p>
          Everything here is public and stays public. If this project ever grows a login,
          it will gate quota, not understanding. You should be able to decide whether
          this is useful without creating an account.
        </p>

        <h2 id="idea">The idea</h2>
        <p>
          Every food API models substitution as a link between two ingredients: butter
          can be replaced by coconut oil. That model is wrong, and it is wrong in a way
          that breaks quietly.
        </p>
        <p>
          A substitution is not a property of a pair. It is a <b>fact about a
          context</b>. Butter to coconut oil is correct in a cookie, tolerable in a cake,
          and ruinous in a croissant. The same two ingredients, three different answers.
          A data model that stores one row per pair cannot express that, so it silently
          returns the optimistic case and your users find the failure for you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Swaprika stores one row per <i>claim</i>. The same pair appears as many times
          as there are distinct situations worth recording. Buttermilk to milk plus acid
          appears twice: once as a leavening reagent for pancakes, where the acid has to
          react with baking soda, and once as a tenderiser in a fried chicken brine,
          where it does a completely different job and fails differently.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <b>The consequence for the API:</b> the useful information lives on the
          relationship, not on either endpoint. In GraphQL terms, it lives on the
          connection edge. That is why the schema looks the way it does.
        </div>

        <h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h2>
        <p>No key required. This works right now:</p>
        <pre><code>{`curl -s https://swaprika.nagysolution.com/graphql \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"query":"{ ingredient(id:\\"butter\\") { substitutions(context:{ dishType: CAKE, requires:[VEGAN] }) { edges { node { canonicalName } ratio { amount basis note } losesRoles } } } }"}'`}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Or open the <a href="/graphql">playground</a>, which loads with a working query
          already in the editor.
        </p>

        <h2 id="auth">Keys and plans</h2>
        <p>
          Send a key in the <code>x-api-key</code> header. No header means the free tier.
          During the preview the demo keys are public on purpose, so you can see both
          tiers without talking to anyone:
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Plan</th><th>Gets</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>free-key</code> or none</td><td>FREE</td><td>Ratios, roles, dietary impact, availability</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dev-key</code></td><td>DEVELOPER</td><td>Plus effects, adjustments, reliability, exclusions</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pro-key</code></td><td>PRO</td><td>Plus sources</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Entitlements are enforced per field, not per endpoint. A free key asking for a
          paid field gets a normal GraphQL response where the permitted fields resolve
          and the paid one is <code>null</code> with an explicit error in
          <code>errors</code>. Nothing is silently omitted.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          These keys are placeholders. Real issuance, hashing and rate limiting land
          before anything is charged for. The schema does not change when they do.
        </div>

        <h2 id="model">How the model works</h2>
        <p>Every substitution edge answers four questions.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>ratio</code></td><td>How much, and <b>on what basis</b>. A bare number is a bug: butter to oil is 0.8 by weight and something else by volume.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>preservesRoles</code> / <code>losesRoles</code></td><td>What culinary job survives the swap and what does not. The second list is the useful one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>adjustments</code></td><td>What you must change to compensate. Swapping sugar for honey without reducing the liquid and dropping the oven temperature gives you a burnt, wet cake.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>validFor</code></td><td>Where it works, and where it is recorded as failing.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="three-states">Three states, not two</h2>
        <p>
          When you ask about a context, the answer is one of three things, and conflating
          the last two is how these APIs mislead people.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Works</td><td>Positively verified in this context</td><td>The edge, with full detail</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fails</td><td>Positively verified to fail here</td><td>Empty, and the context appears in <code>failingTechniques</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unknown</td><td>Never tested</td><td>Empty, and the context appears nowhere</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Filtering is an <b>allow list</b>. A substitution is returned for a context only
          if something positively records that it works there. An earlier version used a
          deny list and happily recommended coconut oil for lamination, purely because
          nobody had written down that it fails. Absence of a recorded failure is not
          evidence of success.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use <code>availableContexts</code> to tell the states apart before you ask, so
          a UI can grey out what is genuinely unknown while still offering the contexts
          that are known to fail. It is a free tier field, because knowing which
          questions are answerable should never be behind a paywall.
        </p>
        <pre><code>{`{
  ingredient(id: "heavy-cream") {
    availableContexts(requires: [VEGAN]) {
      techniques          # verified to work
      dishTypes
      failingTechniques   # verified to fail, still worth asking
      failingDishTypes
      total
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

        <h2 id="chains">Why there are no substitution chains</h2>
        <p>
          A recursive substitution query looks impressive: butter to coconut oil to
          avocado oil to olive oil. It is also nonsense, and it will not be built.
        </p>
        <p>
          Substitutability does not compose. If A works for B in one context, and B works
          for C in another, nothing follows about A and C in either. Two hops from butter
          lands somewhere no cook would go. Recursion is exposed on the taxonomy instead,
          where the hierarchy is real.
        </p>

        <h2 id="schema">Schema reference</h2>
        <p>The types you will actually use. The playground has the full schema.</p>
        <pre><code>{`type Ingredient {
  id: ID!
  canonicalName: String!
  names(locale: String): [LocalizedName!]!   # cornflour resolves to Cornstarch
  roles: [CulinaryRole!]!
  allergens: [Allergen!]!
  parent: Ingredient                          # taxonomy, recursion lives here
  children: [Ingredient!]!
  availableContexts(requires: [DietaryConstraint!]): AvailableContexts!
  substitutions(context: SubstitutionContext): SubstitutionConnection!
}

input SubstitutionContext {
  technique: Technique
  dishType: DishType
  requires: [DietaryConstraint!]
  avoidAllergens: [Allergen!]
}

type SubstitutionEdge {
  node: Ingredient!
  ratio: Ratio!                     # free
  preservesRoles: [CulinaryRole!]!  # free
  losesRoles: [CulinaryRole!]!      # free
  dietary: DietaryImpact!           # free
  validFor: SubstitutionScope!      # DEVELOPER
  effects: [Effect!]!               # DEVELOPER
  adjustments: [Adjustment!]!       # DEVELOPER
  reliability: Reliability!         # DEVELOPER
  sources: [Source!]!               # PRO
}`}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>reliability</code> is an enum (<code>ESTABLISHED</code>,
          <code>SITUATIONAL</code>, <code>CONTESTED</code>) rather than a confidence
          float. There is no model behind a number like 0.91, so publishing one would be
          fake precision.
        </p>

        <h2 id="limits">Limits and honesty</h2>
        <p>
          This is early. The dataset is 21 hand written substitution facts across 45
          ingredients, with 28 recorded failure cases. Nothing is scraped.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Coverage is thin, and biased towards baking and European and North American technique.</li>
          <li>No nutrition data yet, and no branded products.</li>
          <li>Open Food Facts is deliberately excluded from the core, because ODbL share alike would entangle the proprietary substitution data.</li>
          <li>Rate limiting and real key issuance are not implemented yet.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The one promise: if a context has not been tested, the API returns nothing
          rather than guessing. A wrong answer delivered confidently is worse than no
          answer, because you cannot tell it apart from a right one.
        </p>

        <h2 id="licence">Licence</h2>
        <p>
          The server code is MIT. The curated substitution dataset is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,
          which means non commercial use is free with attribution, and commercial use
          needs a separate licence. Both live in the
          <a href="https://github.com/n3ndor/swaprika">repository</a>, along with the
          architecture decision records explaining why each of the choices above was
          made, including the ones that were wrong first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
